    "result_text": "The full suite is green. Let me summarize the change.\n\n## Summary\n\nImplemented the GOLD membership tier rule: `membershipTier` becomes `'GOLD'` when the owner's household (owners sharing the same `householdId`) has 3+ members after the create; otherwise the existing SILVER/BRONZE rules apply.\n\nFollowing the codebase's established pattern for create-time snapshots (`namesakeCount`, `bulkSignupWarning`), I stored the household size as a snapshot on the entity at create time and let the mapper derive the tier from it:\n\n1. **`Owner` entity** (`model/Owner.java`) \u2014 added a `householdSize` field with `@Column(name = \"household_size\")` plus getter/setter.\n\n2. **Schema** \u2014 added the `household_size` column to all four `schema.sql` files (hsqldb, h2, mysql, postgres); tests run on hsqldb.\n\n3. **New pipeline step** `AssignOwnerHouseholdSize` \u2014 a small single-responsibility function that counts existing owners sharing the new owner's `householdId` plus one for the owner being created (not yet persisted), and stores it. An owner with no household id counts as a household of one.\n\n4. **POST pipeline** (`owners.POST.yml`) \u2014 wired the new step in after `assignHousehold` (so the household id is already assigned and backfilled) and before `assignNamesakeCount`, within the create transaction.\n\n5. **`OwnerMapper.membershipTier`** \u2014 returns `'GOLD'` when `householdSize >= 3`, otherwise falls through to the unchanged SILVER/BRONZE logic.\n\nThe new acceptance test (`coreGoldForThreeMemberHousehold`) and the full suite pass (`BUILD SUCCESS`).",
